<%- include('layout-start.ejs', { title: 'PanelsDCC Wi‑Fi Setup', basePath, activeNav: 'getting-started' }) %>
<div class="main-container">
        <div class="getting-started-layout">
        <%- include('sidebar-docs.ejs', { basePath, step: 2, manualChapter: 1 }) %>
        <div class="getting-started-layout-main">
        <div class="full-width-section">
            <h1>Configure Wi‑Fi</h1>
            <aside class="doc-tip-banner" role="note" aria-label="Home users">
                <p><strong>Only ever use your layout at home?</strong> If Wi‑Fi was already set in Raspberry Pi Imager and your network has not changed, you probably do not need to do anything here. The steps below matter most for hotspot mode (e.g. when away from home), or to join new networks.</p>
            </aside>

            <h2>Step 1: Open Connect, then the Wi‑Fi tab</h2>
            <p>On a phone, laptop, or the Pi (same network as the Pi), open Connect below. In the Connect window, select the <strong>Wi‑Fi</strong> tab — that is where the rest of this page happens.</p>
            <%- include('local-panels-urls.ejs', {
                localPanelsHeading: 'PanelsDCC Connect',
                localPanelsLead: 'Port 9000 — after it loads, use the Wi‑Fi tab (not Control).',
                localPanelsShowControl: false,
                localPanelsEmphasis: 'connect'
            }) %>

            <h2>Step 2: Client mode or hotspot mode</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Client mode:</strong> the Pi joins a Wi‑Fi network you’ve saved (for example your home layout network).</li>
                <li><strong>Hotspot mode:</strong> the Pi creates its own hotspot so phones and tablets can connect directly — handy at shows or when there is no trusted network.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>When you leave the area of your saved networks, Connect will <strong>automatically switch to hotspot mode</strong>.</p>

            <h2>Step 3: Hotspot QR code</h2>
            <p>Connect shows a <strong>QR code</strong> so camera-equipped devices can join quickly. Consider printing it and keeping it on or near the Pi case.</p>
            <div class="doc-image-center">
                <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/wifi-mode.png" alt="PanelsDCC Connect Wi-Fi mode screen with hotspot QR code" style="width: 220px;"/>
            </div>

            <h2>Step 4: Add or update known networks</h2>
            <p>From the Wi‑Fi tab, scan for nearby networks, pick one, and enter the password to save it as a <strong>known network</strong> for later sessions. Update this list when your venue or home network changes.</p>
            <div class="doc-image-center">
                <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/wifi-join.png" alt="PanelsDCC Connect Wi-Fi mode screen showing how to join a network" style="width: 400px;"/>
            </div>

            <%- include('getting-started-pager.ejs', {
                pagerPrevHref: basePath + '/getting-started/installation.html',
                pagerPrevTitle: 'Installation',
                pagerNextHref: basePath + '/getting-started/connect.html',
                pagerNextTitle: 'Connecting your DCC controller/s'
            }) %>
        </div>
        </div>
        </div>
    </div>
<%- include('layout-end.ejs') %>
